WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.700
Thank you for the brilliant and astonishingly comprehensive

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:06.680
After seeing it, I went on Twitter

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.280
and asked eligible American voters

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.720
who don't intend to vote

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.740
what their reasoning was,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.740
and today I wanna respond to the six concerns I heard the most.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.100
number one by far:

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.660
"I do not wanna choose between the lesser of two evils"

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.220
Two things; first in most states there will be more than two candidates on the presidential ballot.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.980
secondly, I think maybe you do wanna choose between the lesser of two evils.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.900
you know, because it's the less evil choice.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.680
By not voting you're essentially ranking the candidates as equally evil

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.860
which I think is technically impossible

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.600
given their vastly different policy positions

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.740
Also, you're not just voting for President

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.060
I know it might feel that way

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.340
because of national media coverage

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.680
but the elections down the ballot are extremely important

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.120
It's not just your senators and US congresspeople

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.380
It's your state legislators who make the laws in your state,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.780
the school board members, who decide what the curriculum is gonna look like in your schools.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.120
Those elections have a massive effect on the community you live in.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.460
Okay, concern number two:

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.780
"I am not informed on the issues and/or don't care"

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.500
So if you don't care who your elected representatives are,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.580
we agree on this one,

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.640
you should not vote.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.480
But it's not really that hard to get well informed,

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:05.980
you can go to:

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:08.820
Find your state.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.000
Find the sample ballot

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.300
then you can go to the candidates' websites

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.760
or if it's a race for US Senate or Congress,

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.160
there's a great website called Ballotpedia

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.040
link in the Dooblydoo

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.060
and if you want us to make videos about candidates'

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.460
policy plans and proposals,

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.080
let us know in comments what topics you'd like for us to explore

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:22.960
Concern three:

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.840
"I'm a Republican in a 'blue state' or a Democrat in a 'red state', so my vote doesn't matter"

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.660
First, your down-ballot votes might matter

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.980
but I'd argue your vote matters regardless

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.080
like, you're right that your vote isn't gonna tip the election

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.320
but by not voting you're making politicians in your state believe

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.920
that their state is less politically diverse than it actually is

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.680
and I think that's dangerous because a strong minority has a moderating effect on the party in power

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.380
both at national and local levels

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:49.080
and God knows we could use some moderation

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.100
Concern four:

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.000
"the system is corrupted by money and votes don't actually matter"

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.680
I agree that the system is corrupted by money

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.080
I just don't think we're gonna fix it by not voting

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.460
because not voting out of protest looks, to the system,

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.180
exactly the same as not voting out of apathy

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.680
Also, I don't think money negates the power of your vote

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.700
and I think rich people know this actually

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.500
like, 57.5% of eligible American voters, voted in the 2012 election.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.100
99% of millionaires did, literally.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.200
Number five: illness or disability or transportation problems

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.420
One of my many frustrations with US voting laws is that

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.040
in lots of states it's really difficult to vote if you are sick or disabled.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.520
But find your state in the links below and check out your options,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.020
there usually are some.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.240
Also, if you need a ride on election day

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.600
most local political parties will pick you up and take you to the polls even if you're not voting for their candidates.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.100
Okay, lastly, number six:

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.140
"I am overwhelmed with the process of registering

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.880
and have never voted before, and find this whole thing intensely scary"

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.280
Yeah, I can relate

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.980
like 16 years ago in August of 2000

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.500
I'd just moved to Chicago,

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.080
but I didn't know where I technically lived.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.680
Like, I was sleeping in a walk-in closet of a Chicago apartment

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.660
that my name wasn't on the lease of

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.880
but my driver's license said I lived with mom and dad

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.800
back in Florida, where most of my stuff was

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.240
and it was a stressful and overwhelming time,

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.780
and I had no idea how to register

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.120
and suddenly I looked up and the deadline had passed.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.020
It's true that the system makes it hard for people who move around a lot to vote,

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.440
but I lacked the fortitude to stand up to that system,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.620
and it is one of the great shames of my life.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.840
I feel like I failed my community and myself,

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.960
and also people who live far away from me

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.880
who desperately want the opportunity that I took for granted.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.420
Of course my vote wouldn't have changed the outcomes in any elections

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:23.140
but the combined votes of even 10% of the thousands of people who were like me

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.240
that would've changed some outcomes

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.060
If you're eligible to vote in the United States

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.440
I'm asking you to become a registered and informed voter,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.440
and to make your voice heard,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.860
even if it's hard.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.600
In fact, especially if it's hard.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.720
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

